/*
  KewlCam v2 — ESP32-CAM  (SD-first capture, 3× WiFi, browser-to-Telegram)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Board     : AI Thinker ESP32-CAM (PSRAM required)
  Core      : esp32 Arduino 3.x (2.x guarded where it costs nothing)
  Partition : Minimal SPIFFS (1.9 MB APP / OTA / 190 KB SPIFFS)
  OTA       : curl -F "image=@firmware.bin" http://kewlcam.local/update
  SD        : onboard microSD, 1-bit SDIO (GPIO 14 CLK / 15 CMD / 2 D0)
  Flash LED : GPIO 4, PWM-dimmed (full brightness browns out the SD card)
  Button    : GPIO 13 → GND, hardware interrupt, handled by its own task

  ── WIRING NOTE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Do NOT put the button on GPIO 0. GPIO 0 is XCLK — the 20 MHz clock the
  ESP32 feeds to the OV2640. Grounding it stops the camera and, at power-on,
  puts the chip into flash-download mode. Move the wire to IO13 (GND is two
  pins away on the same header). IO3/U0R also works — see BUTTON_PIN below.

  ── DESIGN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Every photo hits the SD card BEFORE any network work is attempted, so a
  shot is never lost to a dead/slow/absent uplink. Photos that could not be
  delivered leave a marker in /QUEUE; a background task drains that queue
  whenever WiFi returns, and an open browser can drain it over the phone's
  mobile data when the camera itself has no internet. Markers live on the
  card, so the queue survives a reboot or a power cut.

  Three tasks, so a stuck upload can never delay the shutter:
    loop()   core 1  — web server, LED, housekeeping, watchdog
    camTask  core 1  — button/web capture → SD                (prio 5)
    netTask  core 0  — WiFi connect/roam + Telegram uploads    (prio 2)
*/

